cff-version: 1.2.0
message: If you use physverify in your work, please cite it as below.
title: "physverify: verification checks for physics simulation code"
type: software
authors:
  - family-names: Giri
    given-names: Ram Chandra
    orcid: "https://orcid.org/0009-0008-7149-7938"
version: 0.7.0
date-released: 2026-10-31
license: MIT
doi: 10.5281/zenodo.23534512
identifiers:
  - type: doi
    value: 10.5281/zenodo.23534512
    description: Concept DOI, all versions (Zenodo)
repository-code: https://github.com/rcgiri-physics/physverify
url: https://rcgiri-physics.github.io/physverify/
abstract: >-
  physverify checks whether a physics simulation solves the equations it is
  meant to, at the order of accuracy it claims: the method of manufactured
  solutions, observed order of convergence, conserved quantities and budgets,
  exact patch solutions, and Butcher order conditions for Runge-Kutta tables.
  It reports PASS, FAIL, EXACT or INCONCLUSIVE and runs from the command line,
  pytest or GitHub Actions.
keywords:
  - verification
  - method of manufactured solutions
  - order of accuracy
  - numerical methods
  - physics
  - simulation
  - testing
